General Comments: SPAN’s concerns with the proposed PFAS restrictions include:
1. The breadth of the structural definition used to identify PFAS affected by the restrictions;
2. The general presumption of “risk” (without product or use-specific evidence);
3. The presumption that derogations should be time-limited;
4. The absence of permanent derogations for certain critical uses;
5. The absence of a process for renewing time-limited derogations where necessary;
6. The lack of a process for manufacturers and users of PFAS and PFAS-containing products to seek and obtain additional derogations on a going-forward basis;
7. The inclusion of manufactured articles within the scope of the proposed restrictions; and
8. The inevitable negative effect the restrictions will have on global supply chains.

6. Missing uses: SPAN's comments cover concerns over a number of industries which utilize PFAS compounds, including the renewable energy, automotive manufacturing, business equipment, defense and security applications, semiconductor production, medical devices and pharmaceuticals industries.

General Comments: The American Chemistry Council’s (ACC) Performance Fluoropolymer Partnership (hereafter “PFP”) welcomes the opportunity to respond to the call for comments to the Annex XV restriction report with respect to market and end-use segments not covered in the report. The PFP’s members are some of the world’s leading manufacturers, processors and users of fluoropolymers, including fluoroplastics, fluoroelastomers and perfluoropolyethers polymers.

These comments pertain to the use of fluoropolymers (fluoroplastics) in Infrastructure and Construction (I&C) applications. This is a commercial/industrial application/use segment with little to no direct consumer use. This end-use was not called out for special consideration in the EU Restriction Report and therefore would be subject to a full ban at 18 months after entry into force. The fluoropolymers highlighted in this submission are:
 Polyvinylidene fluoride (CAS# 24937-79-9);
 Vinylidene-fluoride, hexafluoro-propene copolymer (CAS#  9011-17-0);
 Ethylene, chlorotrifluoro ethylene copolymer (CAS# 25101-45-5);
 Ethylene, tetrafluoro-ethylene copolymer (CAS# 25038-71-5); and
 Fluoroethylene-vinyl ether copolymer (CBI).

In these comments, we provide compelling case studies and technical data on the use of fluoropolymers in five (5) end-use applications that are often subject to continuous weather extremes based on the climate and/or their physical locations. Those uses are:
1. Building Facades and Protection - Metal Building Panels and Parts;
2. Roofing and Roofing Structures;
3. Bridge and Walkway Structures;
4. Water Towers; and
5. Solar Panels.

We respectfully request a full exemption for the use of fluoropolymers in these particular and related end-uses.

Thank you for the opportunity to provide these comments.

6. Missing uses: The comments attached at Section IV pertain to the use of fluoropolymers (fluoroplastics) in Infrastructure and Construction (I&C) applications.

8. Other identified uses: Please see the detailed comments attached at Section IV.

General Comments: The restriction proposal is not properly supported by adequate data collection:
a. there is no a specific study for each substance, regarding the risks to human health and possible damage to the environment;
b. many of the assumptions are generalized for 10,000 molecules or only hypothesized, although it is stated that the subclasses of molecules have very different behaviors from each other;
c. no assessments have been made to demonstrate that the proposed alternatives to PFAS could worsen environmental impacts;
d. the Manufacture sector is too generic and must be further divided into its specific subsectors;
e. the emissions problem can be solved by better managing the production processes and life cycle of products containing PFAS;

1. Sectors and (sub-)uses: Sectore: Metal plating and manufacture of metal products (Annex E.2.4.)  Subuses: Manufacture of metal products not addressed elsewhere

General Comments: The watch industry uses mixtures containing the substances covered by the draft restriction. These molecules, used in small quantities, are necessary for the production of antispread coatings (hereinafter referred to as "epilames").
Scope of the permanent exemption request:

5. By way of derogation, paragraphs 1 and 2 shall not apply to:
xx. epilame used in watches;
xx. epilame mixtures used in watch manufacturing and servicing;
xx. recycled fluorinated gases making use of a derogation according to paragraph 5 xx) [epilame mixtures used in watch manufacturing and servicing];

For memory, an exemption for epilame was previously suggested in the PFHxA restriction proposal, both by the Dossier Submitter and the SEAC (please refer to ECHA/RAC/RES-O-0000006976-57-01/F, RAC & SEAC opinion on an Annex XV dossier proposing restrictions on undecafluorohexanoic acid (PFHxA), its salts and related substances):
"By way of derogation, paragraphs 1 and 2 shall not apply to epilame-coated internal watch parts and epilame-coatings applied to internal watch parts."

1. Sectors and (sub-)uses: Our comments apply to the following sectors: - Lubricants (sector as a whole); and - Applications of fluorinated gases (solvents).

6. Missing uses: a) Epilames are only used by watchmakers in industrial and professional environment, and are not available to the consumers. The annual consumption of epilames for the whole watchmaking industry is estimated approximately 8 tonnes per year. At the end of the process, solutions are collected and disposed in an appropriate manner. Due to this treatment and to the activated carbon in-situ filtration generally used, the annual release to the environment is estimated below 100 kg per year during the process, the total amount effectively contained in the annual production of watches is under 0.1kg per year.  b) Mechanical assemblies generally require lubrication to function properly. This is particularly the case for timepieces with analogue displays, which means mainly mechanical watches and quartz watches. For this reason, epilames are used and require specific properties such as the capacity to produce ultrathin and transparent coating, a low surface tension, and a chemical compatibility with all materials and lubricants used. Without the use of epilame, the lubricant would spread out of its original location and wear would happen leading to the loss of the watch’s accuracy and thus the dissatisfaction of the consumer.  The fluorinated gas used as carrier fluid require also specific properties such as the chemical compatibility with the epilame, the capacity to solubilize the epilame, a low toxicity, a good wettability, a low risk of use (especially a non-flammability), a low Global Warming Potential, and it should allow to be dry fast.  c) The potential impact in the EU for the analogue watchmaking industry of the submitted restriction under consideration, without exemption, could be estimated at up to 188,000 jobs losses.  d) The use of substances covered by the draft restriction meets a technical need for which there is currently no suitable technical alternative.  e) Analogue watches have a very long lifetime, sometimes even exceeding that of an entire human life, passing from one generation to the next. The consequences of a change in technology must therefore be assessed with such a sustainability objective in mind. By remaining in the same chemical family of molecules, substitution would certainly be simpler, but to be sure that a potential PFAS-based alternative is viable in the long term, it would therefore take years of research and development. And it will be of small impact in terms of benefits for the environment, considering the very small quantities of epilames used.  f) Substitution is not technically feasible.  g) With around 59,000 persons employed in the sector, the Swiss watch industry represents the bulk of the European watch industry. Moreover, it is a major client, possibly the largest, of the European watch component supply industry. Indirectly, tens of thousands of jobs in the retail trade in the European Union are dependent on the Swiss watch industry and the sale of its products. The EU is a traditionally important market for Swiss watch exports. In 2022, the Swiss watch industry exported over six million watches with a total value of CHF 5.4 billion to European Union Member States. These are almost exclusively analogue watches. In 2020, the watchmaking and jewellery industry and downstream sectors employed 167,000 workers in the EU and also 21,000 cross-border worker from EU in Switzerland, considering production, aftersales services and product distribution. These jobs are spread over many companies, mostly SMEs: in Germany only, there are approximately 7,600 enterprises in the retail segment. In the EU, the main manufacturing facilities are located in rural areas such as the Black Forest in Germany and the Jura in France. The potential loss of thousands of jobs would have a particularly hard social impact in these regions. The epilame is a necessity for an analogue watch. As there are tens of millions of watches already on the market, those could not be serviced anymore for oils replacement, leading to a loss of accuracy and autonomy, increase of wear driving to an early obsolescence of the watch and thus economic losses for the owner of the timepiece. The restriction would prohibit the placing on the market of watches and components containing substances covered by the draft restriction. The potential impact in the EU for the analogue watchmaking industry of the submitted restriction under consideration, without exemption, could be estimated at 188,000 jobs losses, €3.0 billion in lost export revenues, and €1.3 billion in lost taxes and social security contributions. When used as an epilame deposited on a component, exposure of the consumer and the environment to these substances is very unlikely, and the quantities used of substances covered by the draft restriction are very small. Considering the financial risks and the major potential impact on the employment of tens of thousands of people, the implementation of a restriction as proposed seems disproportionate without the implementation of the exemption for watchmaking epilames. Both in terms of risk analysis and socio-economic impact.

General Comments: We are a manufacturing company of fire suppression system and core business is to supply extinguishing system to secure critical asset and industries from fire accidents. We are in this business since 1990 and we have experienced all the attempts to search for environmentally sustainable alternatives to halon that had led chemical industry to develop HCFCs, HFCs and finally “last generation” products with low GWP (notably FK-5-1-12 with GWP (100 year) equal to 1) [1].

It is our daily experience to see benefit in using FK-5-1-12 extinguishing system for protecting critical structures such as IT or museums containing sensitive cultural heritage. Available alternatives as water, CO2, inert gases fine solid particle technology (aerosol) have several drawbacks:
- take longer time to extinguish fires (increase of damage to items);
- some are not clean (in term of non-residue forming);
- some can cause health effects;
- some need more space to allocate equipment and agent.

We see with favour the aim of ECHA to reduce PFAS emissions into the environment and make products and processes safer for people, but we believe that proposal should introduce differentiation in PFAS based on their specific properties and use. More in details FK-5-1-12 extinguishing agent should be acknowledged to have the following specific properties with respect to other PFAS:
- it is considered not PBT / vPvB (non-Persistent, non-Bioaccumulable, non-Toxic) [2];
- contribution to TFA (trifluoroacetic acid) and HF (hydrofluoric acid) formation during atmospheric degradation, according to international publication, is not considered harmful at the concentrations expected in the environment [3];
- its use is small, the emissions are low (mainly in the event of fire as leakage monitoring devices are mandatory according to European and international design standard) and rate of recycle is high. European Environmental Agency report an annual emission rate of 3% in 2020 for fluorinated gases in the firefighting sector [4];
- it is currently the only available chemical extinguishing agent not subject to F-Gas regulation [5];
- non-PFAS alternative extinguishing agent do not currently exist and are not in development (even the proposed derogation of 13.5 year after EiF is not likely to be an enough time to identify and develop alternatives).

Based on that we consider appropriate, for FK-5-1-12 extinguishing agent, a time-unlimited derogation.

Sources:
[1] Standard EN 15004-2 “Fixed firefighting system – Gas extinguishing systems – Part 2: Physical properties and system design of gas extinguishing systems for FK-5-1-12 extinguishant” (edition 2020).
[2] Search on https://echa.europa.eu/it/information-on-chemicals/registered-substances with EC / Listing no. 436-710-6.
[3] N. Taniguchi, T. J. Wallington, M. D. Hurley, A. G. Guschin, L. T. Molina, and M. J. Molina, “Atmospheric Chemistry of C2F5C(O)CF(CF3)2: Photolysis and Reaction with Cl Atoms, OH Radicals, and Ozone”, J. Phys. Chem. A, 2003, 107, 2674-2679
[4] European Environmental Agency, Report EEA/PUBL/2022/023. Annual European Union greenhouse gas inventory 1990–2020 and inventory report 2022. Submission to the UNFCCC Secretariat, 27 May 2022.
[5] Regulation EU no.517/2014 https://www.eea.europa.eu/policy-documents/regulation-eu-no-517-2014 .

1. Sectors and (sub-)uses: Fire suppressants
